            <ArticleTitle>Immune hemolytic anemia associated with teicoplanin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>73-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Several drugs can cause immune hemolytic anemia. Here a patient who developed hemolytic anemia after treatment with teicoplanin is described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">Owing to a two-vessel disease, a 68-year-old white man underwent coronary artery bypass grafting. He was readmitted for superficial sternal wound infection and sternal instability. Rewiring was required and worsening anemia characterized the course after the reoperation. Drugs used in the second admission were gentamycin, teicoplanin, paracetamol, and codeine. They were considered as a possible cause of drug-induced hemolytic anemia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The DAT was positive for complement and IgG. Autoanti-e was identified in the patient's undiluted serum sample. The eluate was reactive with all RBCs tested only after adding teicoplanin; when diluted 1:4, anti-e specificity was observed in the presence of teicoplanin.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To our knowledge, this is the first report of immune hemolytic anemia owing to teicoplanin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
